%!PS-Adobe-3.0
%%Title: strokeglyphs.ps — centerline skeleton glyphs for board math
%%Creator: pscat — issue #236
%%EndComments
% @requires: (lib/handscript.ps) run

% Single-stroke skeleton glyphs, handed back as a *path* rather than
% painted: letters, digits, Greek, math operators and growing
% delimiters, for any pen or dry-media tool to follow (docs/DRYMEDIA.md
% section 4, slice G1). `sgpath` appends the jittered centerlines of a
% string to the current path, one subpath per stroke, and paints
% nothing; the caller strokes it with a plain pen, or hands it to a
% medium (drykit's `dkstroke`, #234).
%
%   (x = 2y + 1) 72 600 << /Size 40 /Seed 7 >> sgpath
%   1 setlinecap 1 setlinejoin 2 setlinewidth stroke
%
% Why not handscript: `lib/handscript.ps` is a Type 3 font. Its glyphs
% reach the page through `show` and paint ink, `build.rs` can't catalog
% a Type3Face, and its jitter reads the caller's `rand`. This file
% borrows handscript's lowercase, digits and punctuation at load (one
% copy of that data) without changing a byte of handscript's output.
%
% --- Glyph format -----------------------------------------------------
% Design grid 1000 per em, as in handscript: baseline 0, x-height 500,
% cap height 700, descender -250, math axis 250 (`sgaxis`). At run time
% every glyph is
%
%   [ advance  [llx lly urx ury]  [x y x y ...]  [x y x y ...] ... ]
%
% Each stroke is a flat polyline, drawn in the writer's order and
% direction, and smoothed by handscript's midpoint rule: interior
% points are quadratic control points and segments end at midpoints.
% Two consequences for anyone authoring glyphs:
%   - a curve does not pass through its interior points; look at a
%     /Jitter 0 render, not at the numbers;
%   - a point written twice is a sharp corner (the segment into it and
%     the segment out of it are both straight).
% Glyphs are authored without the bbox (`sgsrc`); `sginit` computes it
% from the points at load. The midpoint curve stays inside the hull of
% its points, so the box bounds the unjittered centerline. Ink extends
% past it by half the pen width plus the jitter.
%
% --- Text ---------------------------------------------------------------
% Text is a string or an array of glyph names. A string is decoded as
% UTF-8: ASCII maps to handscript's lowercase, digits and punctuation
% and to the new capitals and operators; Greek and math symbols map by
% code point (`sgcmap`). Math-hand variants have names only (`/x.curly`
% `/z.crossed` `/y.math` `/t.math` `/n.math`) and are also reached by
% the math-italic code points U+1D465 and friends. A glyph that isn't
% known advances as `.notdef` (300 units) and draws nothing, the same as
% handscript.
%
% --- Jitter -------------------------------------------------------------
% Resolved once into data from a private generator (a Schrage LCG, the
% paintkit `pjrand` pattern, seeded by a hashed /Seed + i). The glyph at
% index i uses seed + i, so editing later text never re-rolls earlier
% glyphs, and the caller's `rand` stream is never touched. Without a
% /Seed the base comes from the origin's device-space position, so the
% same word written in two places differs, and a redraw in place agrees. Each glyph
% gets a baseline drift and shear, and each point an offset, all
% bounded by /Jitter. /Jitter 0 gives the exact skeleton.
%
% Scratch names: every name this file defines starts with `sg`.

% ------------------------------------------------------------- dials

% @kind: Dial
% @summary: The math axis height in strokeglyphs' 1000-per-em design units (250): where fraction bars, minus signs and the centres of growing delimiters sit. Multiply by Size/1000 for points.
% @example: (lib/handscript.ps) run (lib/strokeglyphs.ps) run sgaxis 40 1000 div mul
/sgaxis 250 def

% ---------------------------------------------------------- the data

